## CREATIVE-SOLVE (the skill, run on: "what in this material, taken at its full strength, already crosses or comes nearest to crossing the crossover, and what single missing step would complete the crossing?")

- Gap, exactly: SU(2) is proved uniform-in-volume to β = 1/9 (0.1309 conditional); the crossover is at the resolution-one coupling β ≈ 2.3; the material's own reduction (785078212 8-bis) needs (A) one criterion at the crossover action S* with a margin and (B) flow control from the fine places landing inside that margin.
- Constraints classified: "the window needs blocks of a correlation length, exponential cost" = resource, already broken on the page (COST, treewidth not volume); "the one-link criterion dies at 0.131" = the method's physics, not the theory's; "MK is group-blind" = physics of MK (13dc15ab2) — so no MK-only route; "the flow from β = ∞ to the crossover must be controlled" = physics of the continuum limit (Balaban's remainder, "the part no one has", 16:10) — not breakable; "a finite subgroup cannot stand in for SU(2)" (f0984d270, Petcher–Weingarten freezing) = TRUE IN THE UV, BELIEF AT THE CROSSOVER SCALE: freezing is a weak-coupling fact, while at β ≈ 2.3 the tree already measures BI tracking SU(2) (C_BI/C_SU(2) = 0.9713 at 2.3; the 11-design property decided in the kernel by Design.lean; the residue computable per observable, 4.1 % on TV).
- The binding constraint broken: the finite-subgroup exclusion was applied at every scale; it binds only in the UV. At the ONE scale where the crossing is read, BI is an exact, kernel-checked completion of SU(2) with a measurable residue.
- The single missing step, at the material's own full strength: A CERTIFIED BLOCK (Dobrushin–Shlosman / Weitz row-form, Block.lean's theorem) CRITERION BELOW ONE, WITH A MARGIN, FOR THE SU(2) WILSON ACTION AT A COUPLING WHERE ξ < a (β ≈ 2.0–2.3), read exactly on BI by the transfer organization along one direction (its 9 irreps) with the design residue of THAT functional certified — the "one criterion for S* with a margin" in its first computable form. Runner-up: the same criterion directly on SU(2) by character-basis tensor contraction with certified truncation (ΑΛΟΓΟΝ's Bessel tier); BI wins because its arithmetic is exact in ℤ[√5] and its bridge is already in the kernel.
- What it would and would not complete: it would be a uniform-in-volume gap for 4D lattice SU(2) inside the crossover region — the window [0.131, 2.3] crossed in the lattice sense. It would NOT by itself give the continuum gap: (B), the fine flow landing in that margin, stays the open core the page names.
- First concrete step: run the block criterion at the Z2 and U(1) controls first (law 16), measuring how close to β_c = 0.4407 (Z2) a side-2/3 block reaches. That calibrates the method's loss factor before spending on BI at 2.3.
- Pre-mortem: it fails if the worst-case criterion at every reachable block reads above one at β ≈ 2.3 — the one-link criterion is already 18× short, the star gained 7 % on the control, decimation doubled the region. So "(the block criterion is easily true there)" is the untested assumption to test first. It also fails if BI's residue on the block functional exceeds the margin, or if the margin cannot absorb the fine-flow remainder.
